<subsection xml:id="subsec-partial-derivatives">
    <title>Partial Derivatives, Gradient, and Differentiability</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            For a function of a single variable, the derivative measures the rate of change in the
            only available direction.
            For a function of several variables we can still measure rates of change, but
            now we must specify a direction.
            The simplest choice is to vary one variable while holding all others fixed:
            this gives the <em>partial derivative</em>.
            Collecting all partial derivatives into a single vector yields the <em>gradient</em>,
            which encodes both the magnitude and direction of steepest increase.
            When the function can be approximated near a point by a linear function
            involving the gradient, we call it <em>(totally) differentiable</em> at that point.
        </p>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ─── Partial derivatives ─── -->

    <remark xml:id="rem-partial-derivative-idea">
        <title>Idea of Partial Differentiation</title>
        <p>
            For a single-variable function <m>z = f(x)</m>, the derivative at <m>x_0</m> is the
            limit of slopes of secant lines:
            <me>f'(x_0) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x_0 + h) - f(x_0)}{h}.</me>
            This generalises to a function <m>f(x,y)</m> of two variables as follows:
            fix <m>y = y_0</m>, consider the curve of intersection
            <m>z = f(x, y_0)</m> in the <m>xz</m>-plane, and compute its slope:
            <me>
                \frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(x_0, y_0)
                = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x_0 + h, y_0) - f(x_0, y_0)}{h}.
            </me>
            This is the <em>partial derivative of <m>f</m> with respect to <m>x</m></em> at
            <m>(x_0, y_0)</m>.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-partial-derivative-idea">
            <caption>
                To compute <m>\partial f/\partial x</m> at <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>, we fix <m>y=y_0</m>, intersect the surface with the vertical plane <m>y=y_0</m>, and then take the slope of the tangent line to the resulting curve.
            </caption>
            <image width="84%">
                <latex-image>
